Four design ideas were taken from nyldn/claude-octopus
(https://github.com/nyldn/claude-octopus), MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 nyldn:

  - a fail-fast prompt-size preflight (their `fail` branch only; their default
    strategy summarizes an oversized prompt, and findings from a summarized diff
    cannot be checked against the code)
  - passing prompts by file rather than argv
  - capturing a provider's stdout to a FILE rather than through a pipe or `tee`.
    Their comment records why: provider CLIs spawn helpers that outlive the main
    process while holding the inherited stdout, so a reader waiting for EOF hangs
    on a run that has already finished
  - inferring liveness from output growth rather than from parsed events, so the
    watchdog stays correct across CLI versions that rename their event types

All four were reimplemented rather than copied; no code from that project is
included.
